Because a String cannot be changed in place, convert it to a char array and swap characters from the two ends moving towards the middle. When the two pointers meet, the array holds the reversed text, and you build a new String from it.

```java
public String reverse(String input) {
    char[] chars = input.toCharArray();
    int left = 0, right = chars.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        char temp = chars[left];
        chars[left] = chars[right];
        chars[right] = temp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
    return new String(chars);
}
```

This runs in O(n) time and O(n) extra space for the char array. In real code you would just use new StringBuilder(input).reverse().toString(), but interviewers ask for the manual version to check that you can work with indexes and swapping. Mention the empty-string and single-character cases: both return unchanged, with no special handling needed.

Split the sentence into words, then count each word in a HashMap. getOrDefault keeps the counting to one line: look up the current count (0 if the word is new), add one, and store it back.

```java
public Map<String, Integer> countWords(String sentence) {
    Map<String, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
    for (String word : sentence.toLowerCase().split("\\s+")) {
        counts.put(word, counts.getOrDefault(word, 0) + 1);
    }
    return counts;
}
```

For the sentence "java is fun and java is powerful", this returns {java=2, is=2, fun=1, and=1, powerful=1}. Lowercasing first stops 'Java' and 'java' being counted separately. Note the limits: punctuation stays attached to words ('fun,' is not 'fun'), so a stricter version would strip punctuation first, and the map does not preserve the order words first appeared in — use a LinkedHashMap if order matters.

A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards, so compare characters from both ends moving towards the middle. The first mismatch means it is not a palindrome; if the pointers meet with no mismatch, it is one.

```java
public boolean isPalindrome(String input) {
    int left = 0, right = input.length() - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        if (input.charAt(left) != input.charAt(right)) {
            return false;
        }
        left++;
        right--;
    }
    return true;
}
```

This is O(n) time and O(1) extra space. For a case-insensitive check, compare Character.toLowerCase on each side instead of the raw characters. The same two-pointer idea works for a number: convert it to a string first, or reverse its digits arithmetically and compare with the original — but watch out for overflow and for negative numbers, which are never palindromes because of the minus sign.

Walk the array once, keeping track of the largest value seen and the second-largest distinct value. When a number beats the largest, the old largest becomes the second-largest; when it sits between the two, it becomes the new second-largest. Duplicates of the largest are ignored, so [9, 9, 7] gives 7, not 9.

```java
public int secondHighest(int[] nums) {
    Integer first = null, second = null;
    for (int n : nums) {
        if (first == null || n > first) {
            second = first;
            first = n;
        } else if (n != first && (second == null || n > second)) {
            second = n;
        }
    }
    if (second == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Need at least two distinct numbers");
    }
    return second;
}
```

This is O(n) time and O(1) space. The null-based tracking avoids sentinel-value bugs. If the input is guaranteed to have two distinct values you can drop the exception, but naming the edge case (fewer than two distinct numbers) is exactly what interviewers want to hear.

Collectors.groupingBy sorts stream elements into buckets by a key you choose, and returns a Map from key to list. Add a downstream collector to transform each bucket — counting() turns each list into a count, and mapping() or summingInt() can summarise it another way.

```java
// How many employees are in each department?
Map<String, Long> byDepartment = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::department, Collectors.counting()));

// Group words by their length
Map<Integer, List<String>> byLength = words.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length));
```

The plain one-argument form groups into lists; the two-argument form applies the downstream collector to each group. groupingBy is an eager terminal operation — it consumes the whole stream and builds the map — and the default map is a HashMap, so groups come back in no particular order. If you need sorted keys, pass a TreeMap supplier with the three-argument overload.

The XOR trick works because XOR is its own inverse: after a ^= b, a holds a^b; b ^= a gives the original a; a ^= b then gives the original b. The arithmetic version does the same idea with addition and subtraction, but a + b can overflow for large ints and silently give a wrong answer, which is exactly the follow-up interviewers want to hear. In real code you would just use a temporary variable, which is clearer and lets the compiler optimise; the trick is only worth showing when the question explicitly forbids a third variable.

```java
public void swap(int[] pair) {
    pair[0] ^= pair[1];
    pair[1] ^= pair[0];
    pair[0] ^= pair[1];
}
```

Note the method takes an array because Java passes primitives by value, so swapping two int parameters inside a method would have no effect on the caller. Also guard the XOR version: if both slots hold the same value it still works, but swapping a variable with itself (a ^= a) zeroes it, so never XOR a slot with itself.

A prime has exactly two divisors, 1 and itself, so 0, 1 and negatives are not prime. The key optimisation is that if n has a divisor larger than its square root, the matching divisor smaller than the square root must also exist, so checking up to sqrt(n) is enough. That turns a linear scan into a square-root scan, which matters for large inputs. A small extra win is to check 2 once, then test only odd divisors.

```java
public boolean isPrime(int n) {
    if (n < 2) return false;
    if (n == 2) return true;
    if (n % 2 == 0) return false;
    for (int i = 3; i * i <= n; i += 2) {
        if (n % i == 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```

Using i * i <= n avoids calling Math.sqrt on every iteration. Mention that this trial division is fine for interview-sized numbers, while real cryptography uses probabilistic tests like Miller-Rabin.

The recursion mirrors the definition directly, which is why interviewers ask for it, but fib(5) already recomputes fib(3) twice and fib(2) three times. The number of calls grows like the Fibonacci sequence itself, roughly O(2^n), so fib(50) is hopeless. The fix is memoisation: store results already computed and reuse them, which drops the cost to linear time. An iterative version with two variables is even simpler and uses constant space, so in production you would never ship the naive recursion.

```java
public long fib(int n) {
    if (n < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("n must not be negative");
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
}
```

Mention the stack too: deep recursion can overflow the stack for large n, and long overflows past fib(92), so for big inputs you would need BigInteger.

Walk the string once and record each character's count in a HashMap. A second pass over the map entries picks out the characters with a count above one. Counting first and filtering second keeps the logic simple and runs in O(n) time with O(k) extra space for k distinct characters. Using a LinkedHashMap instead of a HashMap preserves the order in which characters first appeared, which is a nice touch if the interviewer asks for duplicates in order of appearance.

```java
public Set<Character> duplicates(String input) {
    Map<Character, Integer> counts = new LinkedHashMap<>();
    for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
        counts.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
    }
    Set<Character> result = new LinkedHashSet<>();
    for (Map.Entry<Character, Integer> e : counts.entrySet()) {
        if (e.getValue() > 1) result.add(e.getKey());
    }
    return result;
}
```

Map.merge keeps the counting loop to one line. Mention the edge cases: an empty string has no duplicates, and case matters, so 'A' and 'a' are different characters unless the question says to ignore case.
